---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Due date calculator: estimated due date and how far along you are"
  description="Estimate a due date from the first day of your last period, and see how many weeks pregnant you are today. Naegele’s rule, printed on the page."
>
  <h1>Due date calculator</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Put in the first day of your last period. You get the estimated due date, how far along
    you are today, and the date each trimester turns. Every date here is an estimate, and the
    page explains why below.
  </p>
    <figure class="fig fig--health"><img src="/img/due.webp" alt="A baby in a sun hat floating in a pool ring" width="1200" height="514" decoding="async"></figure>

  <div class="sheet">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="lmp">First day of your last period</label>
        <input type="date" id="lmp" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="cycle">Cycle length in days (28 if unsure)</label>
        <input type="number" id="cycle" inputmode="numeric" min="20" max="45" step="1" value="28" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <button class="btn btn--primary" id="calc" type="button">Work out the due date</button>
    <div id="out" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <p class="result-note">Enter the date and the estimate appears here.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="result-note">
      An estimated due date is a planning date, not a promise and not medical advice. About
      1 baby in 20 arrives on the exact day. Your midwife or doctor’s dating scan, usually at
      8 to 14 weeks, overrides anything a calculator says.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>How the estimate is made</h2>
  <p>
    The standard method is Naegele’s rule, from the 1800s and still the first calculation in
    most clinics: take the first day of the last period, add seven days, subtract three
    months, add a year. That is the same as adding 280 days, which is 40 weeks. It assumes a
    28 day cycle with ovulation on day 14. If your cycle is longer or shorter, this page
    shifts the date by the difference, because ovulation usually moves with the cycle.
  </p>
  <p>
    Weeks are counted from the last period, not from conception. That is why you are called
    two weeks pregnant at conception and four weeks on the day a period is missed. French
    practice counts the same span as semaines d’aménorrhée, weeks since the last period,
    sometimes with two weeks added when speaking of weeks of pregnancy. The arithmetic is the
    same, only the label differs.
  </p>

  <h2>One example, worked by hand</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Working</th><th scope="col">Date</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Last period starts</td><td>The date you enter</td><td class="num">1 Feb 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Naegele’s rule</td><td>Plus 7 days, minus 3 months, plus 1 year</td><td class="num">8 Nov 2026</td></tr>
      <tr class="point"><td>Estimated due date</td><td>Same as adding 280 days</td><td class="num">8 Nov 2026</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Second example: with a last period starting 20 September 2026, on 13 November 2026 the
    pregnancy is 54 days along, which is counted as 7 weeks and 5 days, and the due date is
    27 June 2027.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>How accurate is a due date from my last period?</dt>
    <dd>
      Within about a week for most people with regular cycles, wider if your cycle varies by
      more than a few days or you came off hormonal contraception recently. The dating scan is
      more accurate, which is why clinics use the scan date when the two disagree by more than
      a few days.
    </dd>
    <dt>What if I know the conception date instead?</dt>
    <dd>
      Add 266 days to it. That is 280 minus the 14 days Naegele’s rule assumes between the
      period and ovulation. For IVF, clinics count from the transfer date and the age of the
      embryo, and their date is the one to use.
    </dd>
    <dt>What do the trimester dates mean?</dt>
    <dd>
      The second trimester starts at 14 weeks and the third at 28 weeks, counted from the last
      period. Different books shave a day or two either way. They are signposts, not switches:
      nothing about the pregnancy changes overnight at week 14.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">Sources: Naegele’s rule as set out by ACOG in its guidance on estimating the due date. Page last checked 13 November 2026.</p>

  <script>
    const lmpEl = document.getElementById('lmp');
    const cycEl = document.getElementById('cycle');
    const out = document.getElementById('out');

    function fmtDate(t) {
      return new Date(t).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
    }

    function compute() {
      const lmp = lmpEl.valueAsDate;
      if (!lmp) {
        out.innerHTML = '<p class="result-note">Enter the first day of your last period and the estimate appears here.</p>';
        return;
      }
      const cycle = Math.min(45, Math.max(20, parseInt(cycEl.value, 10) || 28));
      const start = Date.UTC(lmp.getFullYear(), lmp.getMonth(), lmp.getDate());
      const adjust = (cycle - 28) * 86400000;
      const edd = start + 280 * 86400000 + adjust;
      const now = new Date();
      const today = Date.UTC(now.getFullYear(), now.getMonth(), now.getDate());
      const days = Math.round((today - start) / 86400000);
      const weeks = Math.floor(days / 7);
      const rem = days % 7;
      const tri2 = start + 98 * 86400000 + adjust;
      const tri3 = start + 196 * 86400000 + adjust;
      let gaText;
      if (days < 0) {
        gaText = 'That date is still in the future, so there is no week count yet.';
      } else if (days > 300) {
        gaText = 'That date is more than 43 weeks ago. If the pregnancy is still going, the dates need a clinician to look at them.';
      } else {
        gaText = 'Counting from that date, today is week ' + weeks + ', day ' + rem + ' of the pregnancy (' + days + ' days along).';
      }
      out.innerHTML =
        '<p class="result-figure">Due around ' + fmtDate(edd) + '</p>' +
        '<p>' + gaText + '</p>' +
        '<table><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td>Second trimester starts</td><td class="num">' + fmtDate(tri2) + '</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Third trimester starts</td><td class="num">' + fmtDate(tri3) + '</td></tr>' +
        '</tbody></table>' +
        '<p class="result-note">Only about 1 baby in 20 arrives on the exact due date. Most arrive in the two weeks either side of it.</p>';
    }

    document.getElementById('calc').addEventListener('click', compute);
    lmpEl.addEventListener('change', compute);
  </script>
</Base>
